ding  and the second characteristic is the  so-called senescence-associated  secretory phenotype which effectively as  it says  are factors that are secreted by  senescent cells that include  inflammatory factors  that it is thought to help to aid  removal of these senescent cells  so that they can get cleared from the  tissue however if the senescent cells  are not cleared and you end up with this  chronic  secretion of these inflammatory factors  it's the thought that actually this  chronic inflammation could be  deleterious for the tissue and  drive the aging process and so two of  the big questions in the senescent field  are what causes a cell to become  senescent in the first place  and secondly can senecent cells be  selectively killed  and is their removal actually a good  thing and so this first paper i  discussed earlier this year  shows how senescent melanocytes within  the skin  through their sas their secretory  phenotype was driving dna damage in the  telomeres of the surrounding  keratinocytes  and now keratinocytes are the cells  within the skin that are most  proliferative and that help to  regenerate the skin layer  and so this they summarized was thought  to be contributing  to aging of the skin as it may be  contributing to the loss of the  replicative capacity of the  keratinocytes  and so it's not just the skin that you  can find tenescent cells you can also  find senescent cells accumulating with  age within the lung  and also adipose tissue and so the other  major question is  is it beneficial to remove these  senescent cells well this is a question  i addressed earlier this year  where we looked at the study defined p16  high  senescent cell types are indispensable  for male's health span  so in this study they used a clever  genetic approach to remove p16 high  cells  and so p16 is a gene that gets highly  expressed  in senescent cells because it's  important for the cell cycle arrest  however when the team removed these  cells in mice they found that after 12  months the mice were increasingly sick  and had skin ulcerations  and they hypothesized that this is  possibly due to the fact that a lot of  these senescent cells that were getting  cleared were found in the liver  and causing liver fibrosis and so this  did suggest that actually maybe removing  senescent cells isn't a very good idea  however we have to take into account  that this was using a genetic approach  that was specifically targeting  high p16 expressing cells and so this is  very different to  cenolytics such as the satanic and  quercetin that may have different  specificity  for senescent cells and so the key take  homes from this study was that senescent  cells are not all equal and ceneolytics  may have specificity for a specific  subset  and secondly it also raises the  importance of potentially combining  xenolytics  with compounds that could potentially  induce cellular replacement  and to effectively replace the senescent  cells and so this nicely brings me on to  the video i made on unity biotechnology  and their potential synolytic failure  with ubxo101 which they were  administrating to patients with  osteoarthritis of the knee  the aim was to be able to clear  senescent cells that were thought to be  associated with the pain  that patients have when they have  osteoarthritis however in their phase 2  trial  unity biotechnology didn't see  separation of ubxo101 treatment  groups from patients that were given a  placebo at 12 weeks  and so they decided not to advance ub  exo101 to a pivotal study and so in that  video i gave a number of reasons as to  why it may have failed  one of which being the large placebo  effect that they saw but it also maybe  could be due to the fact that they were  removing the senescent cells but not  replacing them  and actually being able to to the  condition and so maybe reversal is  required to alleviate pain and  arthritis and the completely different  analytic approach we saw this year  were cenolitic car t cells and so these  are t  cells that can be redesigned to target  specifically senescent cells  and the way that the authors did this  was first by identifying receptors that  are really highly expressed on senescent  cells that aren't found on normal cells  and the target that they found was the  receptor urakinase type plasminogen  activator receptor  or eupar and so you can redesign t cells  to basically recognize this receptor and  to target the destruction of the  senescent cells  and by using these car t cells in their  mouth models  they could show that these cenolitic car  t cells seem to be effective  and so it could be a good strategy  however the downsides  would be the expense of developing these  car t cells in the first place  and also a lot more work will need to be  done to see if it's safe and effective  in humans  and so now moving on to a slightly  different topic this year we've  uncovered some more information to help  understand the aging process for example  understand why nad plus levels  the key metabolites found within our  cells declines during aging  and so this could be due to either less  nad plus being  generated or more nad plants being  consumed  and there's quite a bit of evidence  supporting the latter for example  an enzyme that uses nad plus cd38  has been shown to increase inactivity  during aging  but what two back-to-back nature papers  showed earlier this year  was to try and understand why cd38  levels were increasing  and what was found was that actually our  lies we're going to cover going back to  senescent cells  was that the sasp of the senescent cells  seemed to be driving this increase in  cd38  activity of macrophages and in the  second study they showed that by  inhibiting  cd38 they could prevent the reduction in  nmn levels and nad plus and so it seems  like the  most optimal solution to being able to  restore nad plus levels  that decline during aging is a  combination of both being able to  increase the supply of nad plus from  precursors but also to try and fix the  sink  and find a way to inhibit the cd38  activity  now also this year we learned a bit more  about nad plus metabolism which is  notorious for being quite complicated  and what was found in this research  study was nrh  known as dihydro nicotinamide is another  precursor  for nad plus and in fact when they gave  nrh to mammalian cells  they found that it seemed to be superior  in terms of increasing nad plus levels  compared to  nr or nmn but most interestingly there  seems to be a parallel pathway  to nr and nmn in terms of being able to  increase nad plus levels  now there's still much work to be done  in terms of understanding this pathway  in particular adenosine kinase is  particularly important  for the conversion of nrh into nad plus  and adenosine kinase is busy trying to  make  atp and so there may be implications as  to boosting this pathway  but as i said there's still much to be  learned about this but it's pretty  interesting  and well definitely makes nat plus  metabolism all the more complicated  but the more interesting and well adding  to that complexity  we also learned this year how nadplus  controls circadian reprogramming  through party nuclear translocation to  counter aging  and so what the study was doing was  trying to understand how nadplus was  actually  regulating the circadian rhythm in  addition to what's already known  as to how the circadian rhythm  influences nad plus  levels throughout the day and so there  seems to be a bi-directional  relationship between nad plus  fluctuations during the day  and circadian reprogramming and so this  really is quite complicated and i can't  really explain it to all here so for  that one  imma take like i would just suggest you  watch the video however work in this  area is super interesting because it  does  seem to suggest that maybe there could  be optimal times in which to take  nad plus supplements to best coordinate  with the circadian rhythm  but again i think this may be something  to look for forward to  more 2021 and beyond but yeah  interesting  anyway the last nad plus related news  worth talking about that came out this  year is the study that looked at nad  plus repletion  being able to rescue female fertility  during reproductive aging  and the major take comes from this study  was basically  suggesting that using nad plus  supplementation to improve fertility  as opposed to using arts so-called  assisted reproductive technologies such  as ivf  and so the difference being that things  like ivf can increase the chances of  fertilization but they don't necessarily  address  the causing problem of the infertility  which could be reduced oversight quality  and so potentially by using nmn as a  precursor as was shown in the study  it could help to enhance the oocyte  quality and hence the fertility rates  however these studies were done in mice  and as the authors say we suggest  supplements should not be taken by women  wishing to become pregnant until further  studies have been completed  to assess whether or not this could  actually be effective for humans  and speaking of supplements and  extending health span we saw earlier  this year a study that showed that  alpha-ketoglutarate a key metabolite  that's involved in a variety of  processes  within a cell was shown to extend  healthspan of both  female and male mice with the reduction  in frailty being more dramatic than the  lifespan extension  and in the study they showed that this  could potentially be mediated through  akg's ability  to reduce chronic inflammation which we  know can drive the aging process  and then a study that came out later  this year used akg in mice as well  to ameliorate age-related osteoporosis  and more importantly they showed that  the way that this was occurring  was through regulating histone  methylations for example they saw that  akg treatment of mesenchymal stem cells  showed a decrease in the levels of  h3k9 trimethylation and h3k27  trimethylation  and these two marks are important for  expression of different genes within a  cell  and so changes in gene expression should  explain the reduced senescence phenotype  seen within these mesenchymal stem cells  and the benefit impact on attenuating  bone loss  and now for a completely different  supplement we've also learnt a bit more  about resveratrol this year from various  different publications  and probably the one that caused the  most distress was this molecular cell  paper  genome-wide screens revealed that  resveratrol induces replicative stress  in human cells  and the reason that's caused some  distress is because resveratrol  is commonly taken as a supplement due to  its potential anti-aging benefits  and this has come from a wealth of  studies that have shown that resveratrol  which targets the cyto insert one that  can activate stress resistance pathways  can extend lifespan in yeast worm and  flies as well as misfired in a high fat  diet  and so in this paper they were trying to  take an unbiased approach to identify  pathways that  are implicated by the addition of  resveratrol and inhibition of dna  replication  and induction of replicative stress  seems to be picked up  however in the study they were using a  much higher concentration  than was used in a previous study that  showed that resveratrol had a genome  protective benefit when you use  satellites  and so i think the taking from this  study is to emphasize  how much more research we still need to  do in order to understand these  different supplements better  and also how important research into the  correct dose is needed as well  and how that could also vary for  different people and so now let's look  at the research that came out this year  that more specifically focused on  reversing aging now first up is a  compound known as  isrib that targets the integrated stress  response pathway  and so israel was used in a recent study  a machine to reverse  age-related memory decline in mice the  rationale behind this mechanism  is partially due to the fact that the  integrated stress response pathway  inhibits protein synthesis and protein  synthesis is required  for long-term memory formation and so  targeting the integrated stress response  could be a viable option  for being able to reverse the loss of  memory that occurs during aging  however i think a lot more work will be  required to really validate these  findings  and to assess the safety however this  year we also had the publication of a  completely different method  to reverse the aging process that came  from the david sinclair lab  whereby they used cellular reprogramming  to restore fission  in old mice the way that they did this  was by injecting  three genes into the mices retina oct4  sox2 and klf4 when the authors damaged  the optic nerve by crushing it  they found that expression of these  three genes could actually regenerate  growth of the axons  preventing the damage in fission and  this was remarkable considering that one  of the first systems to lose  regenerative potential  in aging is the central nervous system  and so hopefully you'll agree that  there's been a lot of really great  research that has come out of 2020  regarding longevity and hopefully that  will continue into 2021 and beyond  now i do realize i haven't covered  everything so let me know in the  comments what was your favorite moments  in terms of longevity research this year  so with that have a great happy new year  see you in 2021 and as always thanks for  listening

